  "verbalization": "Procaine and Scn10a may be linked through a mechanism involving anesthetic agents and their structural similarities. The evidence suggests that Procaine, which resembles Oxybuprocaine and Cinchocaine, binds to Scn10a, a subunit of the voltage-gated sodium channel. This binding is supported by the shared structural features among these compounds, which are all classified under the broader category of benzoate esters and organic aromatic compounds. The lowest common ancestors of Procaine and its analogs point to the class of anesthetic agents and benzoate esters, reinforcing the biochemical relationship. The mechanism appears to involve the interaction of these compounds with the Scn10a subunit, potentially modulating sodium channel activity. The evidence is consistent with a pharmacological interaction that could influence neuronal excitability, though the exact mechanism of action remains to be fully elucidated.",
